  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 42a7ef911f4c60e408989a759d2b9b18, type: 3}
  m_Name: PY_FUNCTIONS
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::CodeEscape.Programming.LessonData
  id: PY_FUNCTIONS
  language: 1
  conceptTitle: Functions and return
  symbol: def
  relatedQuestionIds:
  - PY_08_FUNCTIONS
  - HZ_PY_06_FUNCTIONS
  topics:
  - Functions
  definition: A function is a named, reusable block of code that does one job. You
    write it once and use (call) it whenever you need it.
  howItWorks: Create a function with def, a name, brackets for any inputs (parameters)
    and a colon. The indented lines are the function's body. The return keyword sends
    a value back to the code that called the function. A function with no return
    gives back None.
  exampleCode: "def add_bonus(points):\n    total = points + 5\n    return total\n\nprint(add_bonus(10))"
  exampleExplanation: add_bonus(10) calls the function with points set to 10. It
    works out total = 15, and return sends 15 back, so print shows 15.
  tip: print only shows a value on the screen. return hands the value back to the
    code that called the function.
